\cf0 Step 6) \
Use featureCounts on the transcripts.\
Purpose: To figure out how many times each transcript actually occurs (can be used as a measure gene expression)\
Tool: featureCounts\
Version: Galaxy Version 2.1.1+galaxy0\
Parameters: For input, use output of HISAT2 (BAM file). For the gene annotation file, select \'93a gff/gtf file in your history\'94 and input the StringTie output as the gene annotation file. Then, under \'93Does the input have read pairs?\'94, select \'93Yes, paired end and count them as one single fragment\'94. GFF gene identifier should be set to \'93transcript_id\'94.\
\
Step 7)\
Determine if genes are up or down regulated among the replicates of the two growth conditions (broth vs. plate).\
Purpose: To generate the differential gene expression using the two conditions. A negative log 2(FC) means there was down regulated in the broth compared to the plate and a positive number means that there was up regulation in the broth compared to the plate.\
Tool: DESeq2\
Version: Galaxy Version 2.11.40.8+galaxy2\
Parameters: Set factor name to \'93Growth_Media\'94 and name \'931: Factor level\'94 as Broth and \'932: Factor level\'94 as Plate. For each factor level, select the appropriate count files (BrothReplicate# for Broth and PlateReplicate# for Plate). In advanced options, select Yes for \'93Use beta priors\'94 and for outputs, also select \'93Output normalized counts\'94\
Personal Notes: P value can represent whether or not the change you observe is actually significant => the smaller the P value, the better (less than 0.05) and it is a significant change (reject the null hypothesis). P-adj adjusts for the idea that you are comparing multiple things (focus on this P value).\
\
Step 8)\
Purpose: To filter out large amounts of data, searching for specific values on the DeSeq2 output (any P-adj values less than 0.05).\
Tool: Filter (data on any column using simple expressions)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.1.1\
Parameters: Filter: DESeq2 result file, \'93With the following condition: c7<0.05\'94\
\
Step 9)\
Purpose: To filter out large amounts of data, searching for specific values on the DeSeq2 output (anything with a log2(FC) less than 1).\
Tool: Filter (data on any column using simple expressions)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.1.1\
Parameters: Filter: Previous filter dataset result file, \'93With the following condition: abs(c3)>1\'94\
Plot Interpretations (broadly): From these plots, you can learn how the differing conditions (in this case, broth vs. plate) compare and how they relate to variance in the data. In this example, there was clustering among the replicates for the same conditions (plates were clustered together and broths were clustered together). They can also indicate the extent of up regulation and down regulation.\
\
Step 10)\
Purpose: Create headers for the latest filtered dataset\
Tool: TextEdit\
Version: 1.20 (415)\
Parameters: In a blank doc, add the following, being sure that tab is used to separate (without spaces): GeneID / Base mean / log2(FC) / StdErr / Wald-Stats / P-value / P-adj. Once complete, go to \'93Upload\'94, select \'93Paste/Fetch data\'94. Copt and paste the text into this textbox, name it \'93header\'94, select \'93tabular\'94, and select \'93Start\'94.\
\
Step 11)\
Purpose: Combine the newly created header with the latest filtered dataset\
Tool: Concatenate multiple datasets or collections\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.0.0\
Parameters: Select the Header in the Concatenate Dataset, select \'93Insert Dataset\'94 and the latest filtered dataset. }